# GREP(1)

## NAME

grep, egrep, fgrep - search a file for a pattern

## SYNOPSIS

| **grep** [ **-bchlnsvy** ] [ **-e** ] expression [ file ] ...

| **egrep** [ option ] ... [ expression ] [ file ] ...

| **fgrep** [ **-bchlnsvxy** ] [ **-e** ] [ **-f** file ] [ strings ] [ file ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

Commands of the *grep* family search the input *files* (standard input default) for lines
matching a pattern. Normally, each line found is copied to the standard output; unless the
**-h** flag is used, the file name is shown if there is more than one input file.

*Grep* patterns are limited regular expressions in the style of ed(1); it uses a compact
nondeterministic algorithm. *Egrep* patterns are full regular expressions; it uses a fast
deterministic algorithm that sometimes needs exponential space. *Fgrep* patterns are fixed
strings; it is fast and compact.

Note: *egrep* is not on this system. It is a yacc(1) grammar and there is no *yacc* here yet;
see *cmd/TODO.md* in the source tree.

The following options are recognized.

**-v**
: All lines but those matching are printed.

**-c**
: Only a count of matching lines is printed.

**-l**
: The names of files with matching lines are listed (once) separated by newlines.

**-n**
: Each line is preceded by its line number in the file.

**-b**
: Each line is preceded by the **byte offset** in the file at which it begins, counting from
  zero. This is useful in locating a match by position rather than by line.

> Note: v7 printed a *block* number here - the offset divided by a PDP-11 disk block of 512
> bytes. 512 names nothing on this machine, a block here is 3072 bytes, and *grep* divided by
> that constant where *fgrep* divided by a hard-coded 512, so the two commands answered
> differently about the same match. The division is gone rather than the divisor changed: a byte
> offset is exact, needs no unit, and both commands now agree.

**-s**
: No output is produced, only status.

**-h**
: Do not print filename headers with output lines. This applies to **-c** as well; v7's **-c**
  printed the header regardless.

**-y**
: Lower case letters in the pattern will also match upper case letters in the input. *Fgrep*
  implements this too, and matches without regard to case in both directions; v7's manual page
  said the flag was *grep* only and was wrong about its own program.

> Note: a byte above 0177 is part of a multi-byte letter, which has no case on this system, so
> **-y** passes it through unchanged.

**-e** *expression*
: Same as a simple *expression* argument, but useful when the *expression* begins with a -.

**-f** *file*
: The string list (*fgrep*) is taken from the *file.* This is the only way to give *fgrep* more
  than one keyword from a script, the list being newline-separated. *Grep* has no **-f;** v7's
  manual page implied otherwise.

**-x**
: (Exact) only lines matched in their entirety are printed (*fgrep* only).

Care should be taken when using the characters $ \* [ ^ | ? ' " ( ) and \\ in the *expression*
as they are also meaningful to the Shell. It is safest to enclose the entire *expression*
argument in single quotes ' '.

*Fgrep* searches for lines that contain one of the (newline-separated) *strings.*

*Egrep* accepts extended regular expressions. In the following description `character` excludes
newline:

> A \\ followed by a single character matches that character.

> The character ^ ($) matches the beginning (end) of a line.

> A **.** matches any character.

> A single character not otherwise endowed with special meaning matches that character.

> A string enclosed in brackets [] matches any single character from the string. Ranges of
> character codes may be abbreviated as in `a-z0-9`; see EIGHT-BIT TEXT below for what a range
> means here. A ] may occur only as the first character of the string. A literal - must be placed
> where it can't be mistaken as a range indicator.

> A regular expression followed by \* (+, ?) matches a sequence of 0 or more (1 or more, 0 or 1)
> matches of the regular expression.

> Two regular expressions concatenated match a match of the first followed by a match of the
> second.

> Two regular expressions separated by | or newline match either a match for the first or a match
> for the second.

> A regular expression enclosed in parentheses matches a match for the regular expression.

The order of precedence of operators at the same parenthesis level is [] then \*+? then
concatenation then | and newline.

## EIGHT-BIT TEXT

The text on this system is UTF-8 end to end, and both commands carry eight bits.

*Fgrep* compares bytes and always did, so a keyword may be any text at all.

*Grep* matches **bytes,** which is the same rule ed(1) follows and which has three visible
consequences. A **.** matches one byte and not one letter, so a two-byte Cyrillic letter needs
two of them. A **\*** after a multi-byte letter repeats that letter's *last* byte. And a range
written between two multi-byte letters, as in [**а-я**], is a range of the bytes that spell
them and not of the letters between them.

A bracketed class holds each byte value separately - the table is 256 bits, one per value - so
[**пм**] matches any of the four bytes that spell those two letters.

Note: v7's class table was 128 bits and its matcher masked every input byte with 0177, which
folds the lead byte of a Cyrillic capital onto an ASCII letter: [**п**] would have been found
inside **ALPHA**. Worse, nothing masked the byte on the way *in,* so a class containing a byte
above 0177 stored past the end of its own table and quietly corrupted the compiled expression.
Both are gone.

## SEE ALSO

ed(1), sed(1), sh(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Exit status is 0 if any matches are found, 1 if none, 2 for syntax errors or inaccessible
files. An unknown flag is an error and exits 2; v7 printed a message and then searched for the
bad flag.

## BUGS

Ideally there should be only one *grep,* but we don't know a single algorithm that spans a wide
enough range of space-time tradeoffs.

*Grep* truncates a line at 511 characters and treats the remainder as a new line, so a long
line may be reported more than once and the cut may land inside a multi-byte letter. *Fgrep*
reads through a ring of 1024 characters: a matching line longer than that has had its beginning
overwritten by the time the line is printed, and only its last *length* - 1024 characters come
out. (*v7's* page said 256 for both and was wrong about both. Both limits are v7's own and
neither was changed.)

*Grep* matches a starred expression by recursion, one level per **\*** that consumes anything,
and says **grep: expression too complex** past sixteen levels. An expression anybody writes
goes two or three deep. Note: without that limit the recursion runs off this machine's
4096-word stack - and it returns a *wrong* answer, an empty one, for a dozen levels before it
faults.

*Fgrep* holds its keywords in a table of 3000 states - one state per character of keyword text,
less whatever the keywords share as a prefix - and says **fgrep: wordlist too large** beyond
that. There is no separate limit on the *number* of keywords: five hundred short ones fit
easily, and it takes one keyword of three thousand characters to fill the table on its own. v7
allowed 6000 states, which does not fit this machine's address space: a state is four 48-bit
words here and eight bytes on a PDP-11.

v7 also carried a second ceiling that no manual page mentioned - a 400-entry queue in the
routine that computes the failure links - which stopped *fgrep* at 399 keywords whatever their
length, gave that same diagnostic when it did, and overran its own stack frame for a single
keyword of about 450 characters. The queue is sized from the state table now and cannot
overflow, so the 3000 states above is the only limit there is.

And v7's *fgrep* computed some of its failure links wrongly - it followed one link from the
failing state and then restarted at the root, where the chain of links has to be followed to
its end - so a keyword could go unreported although it was present. Keywords **bd**, **debdb**
and **ebb**, against the line **debd**, is the smallest case: v7 printed nothing. About one
keyword list in a hundred was affected, and the failure was silent - indistinguishable from an
honest absence of matches. Fixed, and checked against 2000 generated keyword lists.
